[32m+[m[32m### Local container hardening[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mAll runtime containers run as non-root users. The gateway and frontend use[m
[32m+[m[32m`nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged` and listen on container port 8080; the browser[m
[32m+[m[32mURL remains http://localhost:8080. Each container has a read-only root[m
[32m+[m[32mfilesystem, all Linux capabilities dropped, and `no-new-privileges` enabled.[m
[32m+[m[32mOnly `/tmp` is writable through a bounded 64 MiB temporary filesystem mounted[m
[32m+[m[32mwith `noexec` and `nosuid`; its contents are disposable. Nginx uses it for its[m
[32m+[m[32mPID and temporary request files. Do not add privileged mode or mount the[m
[32m+[m[32mDocker socket to these services.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mDocker's local log driver rotates logs at 10 MiB per file and retains three[m
[32m+[m[32mfiles per container. Health checks and `deploy/smoke-test.ps1` check local[m
[32m+[m[32mavailability; they are not continuous external monitoring or alerting.[m
[32m+[m[32mRun `deploy/security-test.ps1` to verify runtime isolation and published ports.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mDockerfiles copy only explicitly required application files. Both build[m
[32m+[m[32mcontexts exclude `.env*`; this is an additional safeguard, not a substitute[m
[32m+[m[32mfor secret management. Never put credentials in source, image layers, build[m
[32m+[m[32marguments, or frontend JavaScript. If a future service needs a secret, mount[m
[32m+[m[32mit as a read-only secret file and make that service explicitly read it.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mRefresh base images regularly, rebuild, and verify before accepting updates:[m
[32m+[m
